[16.198601, "o", "\"GEMINI\": \"**The Iliad: A Masculine Epic**\\n\\n* **Focus on War and Heroism:** The Iliad is primarily concerned with the Trojan War and the heroic exploits of Achilles. Battles, sieges, and duels dominate the narrative, and the poem glorifies the warrior ethos and the pursuit of honor and glory.\\n\\n* **Male Dominance:** The Iliad is a predominantly male-centered story. Women play relatively minor roles, and their perspectives and experiences are largely marginalized. The poem's focus is on the actions and motivations of men, and women are often portrayed as objects of desire or pawns in the political and military machinations of men.\\n\\n* **Emphasis on Strength and Physical Prowess:** The heroes of the Iliad are characterized by their physical strength, courage, and skill in battle. Achilles, in particular, is depicted as an almost superhuman warrior, capable of feats of incredible strength and endurance. The poem celebrates the physical prowess and martial virtues of its heroes.\\n\\n* **Themes of Honor and Rev"]
[16.198649, "o", "enge:** Honor is a central theme in the Iliad, and the characters are often motivated by a desire to avenge wrongs done to them or their families. Achilles' anger and subsequent withdrawal from the war are driven by his desire to avenge the death of his friend Patroclus. The poem explores the complex relationship between honor, revenge, and the destructive consequences that can result from an unyielding pursuit of these ideals.\\n\\n**Comparison with the Odyssey:**\\n\\n* **Odyssey as a More Balanced Epic:** In contrast to the Iliad's focus on war and heroism, the Odyssey is a more balanced epic that explores a wider range of themes and experiences. It incorporates elements of adventure, romance, and domestic life, and it delves into the inner worlds and emotions of its characters, both male and female.\\n\\n* **Greater Emphasis on Character Development:** The Odyssey places a greater emphasis on character development and psychological depth. Odysseus, the protagonist, undergoes a profound transformation throughout"]
[16.198657, "o", " the poem as he faces numerous challenges and obstacles on his journey home. The Odyssey explores themes of identity, loyalty, and the complexities of h\r\n\"GEMINI\": \"Yes, the Iliad is frequently described as a masculine or heroic epic, especially when compared with the Odyssey.\"\r\n            }\r\n        },\r\n        \"Date_modification\": {\r\n            \"declare_input\": \"The poem Iliad is frequently described as a masculine or heroic epic, especially compared with the Odyssey.\",\r\n            \"declare_ans\": {\r\n                \"GPT3\": \"Written by Homer, the Iliad is indeed often characterized as a masculine or heroic epic, particularly when contrasted with the Odyssey. This distinction stems from the themes, characters, and overall tone of the two works.\\n\\nThe Iliad is primarily focused on the Trojan War, a conflict that revolves around battles, valor, and the pursuit of glory in combat. Through vivid descriptions of warfare and the actions of warriors, Homer explores the idea of heroism and the code of honor that "]
[16.198662, "o", "governs the characters. The heroes of the Iliad, such as Achilles, Hector, and Ajax, are portrayed as strong, brave, and fiercely committed to their warrior ideals. The poem celebrates their masculine virtues, including strength, courage, and martial prowess.\\n\\nIn contrast, the Odyssey has a more varied and complex narrative, encompassing the adventures of the hero Odysseus as he tries to return home after the Trojan War. While the Odyssey also features elements of heroism, it places greater emphasis on cunning, intelligence, and resourcefulness. Odysseus is presented as a more multidimensional character, navigating challenges through wit and strategy rather than strict physical prowess. Additionally, the Odyssey explores themes of familial love, personal growth, and the consequences of one's actions.\\n\\nThe differing tones of the two poems further contribute to their contrasting characterizations. The Iliad is marked by a somber and intense atmosphere, reflecting the brutality and tragedy of war. Conversely"]
[16.198712, "o", ", the Odyssey is characterized by a more light-hearted and whimsical tone, incorporating fantastical elements and a sense of adventure.\\n\\nWhile it is essential to recognize the Iliad's masculine and heroic nature, it is also worth noting that it contains moments of tenderness, sorrow, and introspection. For ex\r\n\"GEMINI\": \"The Iliad is an ancient Greek epic poem, traditionally attributed to Homer, describing the siege of Troy. The poem is divided into 24 books and contains more than 15,000 lines. The story begins with a quarrel between Achilles and Agamemnon, the leaders of the Greek army, and ends with the death of Hector, the greatest Trojan warrior.\\n\\nThe Iliad is often described as a masculine or heroic epic, especially when compared to the Odyssey, another ancient Greek epic poem attributed to Homer. The Iliad is full of battles, violence, and bloodshed, while the Odyssey is a more personal and introspective story. The Iliad is also more focused on the exploits of individual heroes, such as Achilles, He"]
[16.198824, "o", "ctor, and Odysseus, while the Odyssey is more interested in the journey of a single hero, Odysseus.\\n\\nThere are several reasons why the Iliad is often seen as a more masculine epic than the Odyssey. First, the Iliad is much more focused on war and violence. The poem is full of descriptions of battles, duels, and other forms of violence. The Odyssey, on the other hand, contains less violence and is more focused on the journey of Odysseus.\\n\\nSecond, the Iliad features a number of strong and powerful male characters. Achilles, Hector, and Odysseus are all larger-than-life heroes who are skilled in battle and capable of great feats of strength. The Odyssey, on the other hand, features a more diverse cast of characters, including women and children.\\n\\nThird, the Iliad celebrates the values of honor, glory, and loyalty. The heroes of the Iliad are willing to fight and die for their honor and the glory of their homeland. The Odyssey, on the other hand, is more interested in the value of family and homecoming.\\n\\n"]
[16.198961, "o", "Of course, the Iliad is not simply a story about war and violence. The poem also contains a number of themes that are relevant to both men and women, such as love, loss, and revenge. The Iliad is a complex and multifaceted work that can be enjoyed by people of all ages and genders.\"\r\n            },\r\n            \"question_input\": \"Is the poem Iliad frequently described as a masculine or heroic epic, especially compared with the Odyssey?\\n\",\r\n            \"question_an\r\n"]
[16.201771, "o", "\u001b[?2004h\u001b[01;32magent@95c8f7f39f57\u001b[00m:\u001b[01;34m/app\u001b[00m$ "]
[39.231068, "i", "python - <<'PY'\nimport re, zipfile\nfrom xml.sax.saxutils import escape\nfrom xml.etree import ElementTree as ET\nfrom pathlib import Path\ns=Path('/app/llm_answers.json').read_text()\nresponses=re.findall(r'\"GEMINI\"\\s*:\\s*\"((?:[^\"\\\\]|\\\\.)*)\"',s)\nassert len(responses)==6\nword=re.compile(r'\\bIliad\\b')\nparas=[]\nfor text in responses:\n if paras: paras.append('<w:p/>')\n runs=[]\n pos=0\n for m in word.finditer(text):\n  if m.start()>pos:\n   runs.append('<w:r><w:t xml:space=\"preserve\">'+escape(text[pos:m.start()])+'</w:t></w:r>')\n  runs.append('<w:r><w:rPr><w:highlight w:val=\"yellow\"/></w:rPr><w:t>Iliad</w:t></w:r>')\n  pos=m.end()\n if pos<len(text): runs.append('<w:r><w:t xml:space=\"preserve\">'+escape(text[pos:])+'</w:t></w:r>')\n paras.append('<w:p><w:pPr><w:spacing w:after=\"0\"/></w:pPr>'+''.join(runs)+'</w:p>')\nns='http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/wordprocessingml/2006/main'\ndoc='<?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\" standalone=\"yes\"?><w:document xmlns:w=\"'+ns+'\"><w:body>'+''.join(paras)+'<w:sectPr><w:pgSz w:w=\"12240\" w:"]
[39.231183, "i", "h=\"15840\"/><w:pgMar w:top=\"1440\" w:right=\"1440\" w:bottom=\"1440\" w:left=\"1440\"/></w:sectPr></w:body></w:document>'\nct='<?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?><Types xmlns=\"http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/package/2006/content-types\"><Default Extension=\"rels\" ContentType=\"application/vnd.openxmlformats-package.relationships+xml\"/><Default Extension=\"xml\" ContentType=\"application/xml\"/><Override PartName=\"/word/document.xml\" ContentType=\"application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.wordprocessingml.document.main+xml\"/></Types>'\nrels='<?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?><Relationships xmlns=\"http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/package/2006/relationships\"><Relationship Id=\"rId1\" Type=\"http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/officeDocument/2006/relationships/officeDocument\" Target=\"word/document.xml\"/></Relationships>'\noutput='/app/gemini_results.docx'\nwith zipfile.ZipFile(output,'w',zipfile.ZIP_DEFLATED) as z:\n z.writestr('[Content_Types].xml',ct)\n z.writestr('_rels/.rels',rels)\n z.writestr('word/document.xml',doc)\nwit"]
[39.231546, "o", "python - <<'PY'\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> import re, zipfile\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> from xml.sax.saxutils import escape\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> "]
[39.231789, "i", "h zipfile.ZipFile(output) as z:\n assert z.testzip() is None\n root=ET.fromstring(z.read('word/document.xml'))\n n={'w':ns}\n texts=[''.join(p.itertext()) for p in root.findall('w:body/w:p',n)]\n assert texts[::2]==responses\n assert all(t=='' for t in texts[1::2])\n highlights=root.findall('.//w:highlight',n)\n assert len(highlights)==sum(len(word.findall(t)) for t in responses)\n print('Verified:',len(responses),'responses;',len(highlights),'highlighted occurrences; source escaping preserved.')\n print(output,Path(output).stat().st_size,'bytes')\nPY\n"]
